\f0\b \cf0 3. Customer Segmentation & Emerging Customers
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab560\slleading20\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
In the gutter supply industry, 
\f0\b customers range from specialized gutter companies to general contractors and even DIY consumers.
\f1\b0  Below is a breakdown of key customer groups in Eastern PA and their trends:\
	\'95	
\f0\b Specialized Gutter Installers:
\f1\b0  These are companies 
\f0\b focused primarily on gutter installation and repair
\f1\b0 , sometimes also offering gutter guards and cleaning. Examples include franchise or regional businesses like 
\f2\i The Brothers that Just Do Gutters
\f1\i0  and 
\f2\i The Gutter Guys
\f1\i0 . This segment has been 
\f0\b growing steadily
\f1\b0 , as evidenced by the expansion of franchises and local specialists across Pennsylvania. They typically purchase gutter coil, downspouts, and accessories in bulk and often invest in their own seamless gutter machines. They value suppliers who offer a wide color selection and quick turnarounds. 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  High \'96 These businesses are capturing market share as homeowners increasingly seek dedicated experts for gutter projects. Many are 
\f0\b expanding into new territories (franchise growth)
\f1\b0 , which presents new sales opportunities.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Roofing Contractors:
\f1\b0  Many roofing companies offer gutters as a complementary service (since gutters are commonly replaced during re-roofing). In Eastern PA, a significant number of roofing contractors include seamless gutters in their service lineup. They might not do gutters full-time, but they are a 
\f0\b major customer group for gutter supplies
\f1\b0 . They often prefer one-stop shopping \'96 buying shingles, gutters, and flashing from the same distributors (e.g., ABC Supply or Beacon). 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  Moderate \'96 Roofing contractors\'92 gutter business grows in line with re-roofing activity. With re-roofing demand strong (due to aging homes and recent storms), their gutter material needs remain robust. However, some roofers are content sub-contracting gutters to specialists, depending on the company\'92s focus.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Siding & Exterior Contractors:
\f1\b0  Similar to roofers, companies that do siding, windows, or general exterior remodeling frequently handle gutter replacements on their projects. They may source gutters per job or maintain a small inventory. In the PA/NJ area, exterior remodelers (e.g., 
\f0\b PJ Fitzpatrick
\f1\b0 , a large Delaware-Valley firm, or 
\f2\i The Exterior Company
\f1\i0  in PA) are notable players that buy gutter materials. 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  Steady \'96 These contractors\'92 gutter purchases fluctuate with overall remodeling trends. As home improvement spending in the region remains high (with homeowners investing in exteriors), this segment continues to provide consistent demand for supplies.\
	\'95	
\f0\b General Contractors & Home Builders:
\f1\b0  Residential home builders and general contractors constructing new homes or additions will need gutters as part of delivering a complete project. Within 150 miles of 19606, there are numerous home builders (large and small). Larger builders often buy gutters through sub-contractors or have accounts with big distributors. Smaller custom builders might work directly with local gutter suppliers for specific profiles or colors. 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  Rebounding \'96 New housing starts in PA are picking up, so builders\'92 demand for gutter systems is rising accordingly. This segment is sensitive to economic cycles (interest rates, housing market conditions) but currently represents a growth opportunity as housing construction improves into 2025.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Maintenance & Facility Managers:
\f1\b0  Property management companies, facility maintenance contractors, and even some public entities (school districts, etc.) occasionally purchase gutter materials for direct replacement or repair jobs on their buildings. Often they will still use an installer, but larger complexes might stock spare downspouts or sections. 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  Low \'96 This is a smaller segment. Such customers usually buy on an as-needed basis and might even purchase through retail channels for convenience.\
	\'95	
\f0\b DIY Homeowners:
\f1\b0  A portion of the market consists of homeowners buying gutters or parts from retail stores (Home Depot, Lowe\'92s, or online suppliers like GutterSupply.com) to do small repairs or install sectional gutters themselves. In Eastern PA\'92s older neighborhoods, a handy homeowner might replace a section of gutter or add a short run on a shed. 
\f2\i Growth trend:
\f1\i0  Small but notable \'96 The DIY segment isn\'92t the main revenue driver for wholesale-focused suppliers, but the proliferation of online gutter supply retailers has made it easier for determined DIYers to access materials. This segment tends to favor vinyl or simple aluminum sectional systems due to ease of installation, but some ambitious DIYers will even buy small gutter machines or pre-cut seamless lengths for personal use.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Emerging Customer Types:
\f1\b0  New or evolving customer categories in this industry include:\
	\'95	
\f0\b Franchise Gutter Companies:
\f1\b0  The rise of gutter-only franchise businesses (e.g., The Brothers that Just Do Gutters, which now has multiple locations in PA) is creating a network of well-organized small businesses that often have significant purchasing needs for materials as they ramp up operations. They often seek reliable regional suppliers for steady coil and accessory supply.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Exterior Cleaning Companies Adding Gutter Services:
\f1\b0  Firms that historically only did gutter cleaning (like 
\f0\b Ned Stevens Gutter Cleaning
\f1\b0 , a major player in the Northeast) or power washing are increasingly adding gutter installation and repairs to their services. This means companies that didn\'92t used to buy materials are becoming customers for gutter suppliers. For instance, gutter cleaning businesses might start stocking gutter sections, hangers, and sealants to do minor fixes for their clients.\
	\'95	
\f0\b Online Retailers & E-commerce Customers:
\f1\b0  With more e-commerce options, some contractors are buying certain specialty items online (e.g., a particular gutter guard or a custom color accessory not available locally). This isn\'92t a customer \'93type\'94 per se, but it represents a shift in purchasing behavior \'96 
\f0\b suppliers may find themselves competing with or utilizing e-commerce channels
\f1\b0  to serve tech-savvy customers who expect quick shipping and a broad catalog.\
